  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x-6,\\, & x<1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$\n- $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$\n- $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$",
  "worked_solution": "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =3(1)-6 \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
